235
00:11:46,960 --> 00:11:50,556
And, I think that's a direction
we should push in more.

236
00:11:50,639 --> 00:11:53,159
Not just from the capabilities,
but from a safety perspective too.

237
00:11:53,296 --> 00:11:56,680
I note when you talk about
agent "teams," not "swarms"—

238
00:11:58,080 --> 00:11:58,800
Swarm is such a—

239
00:11:59,320 --> 00:12:00,760
I know, it's locusts.
Yeah.

240
00:12:01,240 --> 00:12:03,000
Not good.
Not good branding.

241
00:12:03,919 --> 00:12:06,475
In keeping with many historical
AI industry traditions.

242
00:12:06,559 --> 00:12:09,439
We are so good at this.
We'll get back to branding.

243
00:12:10,039 --> 00:12:13,639
But, I note that there seems to be

244
00:12:14,360 --> 00:12:17,755
a big gap in what the leading AI users are

245
00:12:17,840 --> 00:12:22,115
doing and standard corporate use,
in the use of swarms,

246
00:12:22,200 --> 00:12:25,040
where like all the advanced math—sorry,
"teams." All the advanced

247
00:12:25,124 --> 00:12:26,440
math uses agent teams.

248
00:12:26,879 --> 00:12:28,919
All the advanced hacking uses agent teams.

249
00:12:29,360 --> 00:12:34,320
And then, common or garden corporate stuff
is not using big swarms.

250
00:12:35,240 --> 00:12:39,279
Why has that not—because we've
had this for many months?

251
00:12:39,759 --> 00:12:41,720
Not to the same degree.

252
00:12:42,360 --> 00:12:46,840
The one way to look at this problem
or this question is: "How much

253
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:51,316
compute can we productively throw
at a single problem?" And...

254
00:12:51,399 --> 00:12:54,600
A few years ago, we forget
how fast this has all gone.

255
00:12:55,120 --> 00:12:57,556
But a few years ago,
it was impossible to throw more than

256
00:12:57,639 --> 00:13:00,379
a dollar of compute at a problem
and have it help you at all.

257
00:13:00,559 --> 00:13:00,904
Why?

258
00:13:02,559 --> 00:13:06,075
You could have GPT-4 grind out for you...

259
00:13:06,159 --> 00:13:08,240
100,000 tokens or something.

260
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:10,460
And it was going to answer your question.

261
00:13:10,600 --> 00:13:13,920
And it certainly was not going to be able
to go write productively, like,

262
00:13:14,004 --> 00:13:16,000
write you a piece of software.

263
00:13:17,039 --> 00:13:17,439
We just...

264
00:13:17,524 --> 00:13:19,916
We had not figured out how to use a lot
of test time compute

265
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:21,360
to get you a better result.
I see.

266
00:13:21,443 --> 00:13:24,559
And then, we figured out chain-of-thought
and the models could think

267
00:13:24,644 --> 00:13:25,755
hard about a problem.

268
00:13:25,840 --> 00:13:27,475
And now...

269
00:13:27,559 --> 00:13:30,235
People really started
using a lot of compute.

270
00:13:30,320 --> 00:13:34,236
And now, you can throw
a whole team at something.

271
00:13:34,320 --> 00:13:36,879
And you can do that
10,000 times in parallel.

272
00:13:37,039 --> 00:13:39,795
You need chain-of-thought and test-time
compute before you can get to swarms.

273
00:13:39,879 --> 00:13:42,275
Because you can't have a useful
swarm with a bunch of GPT-3s.

274
00:13:42,360 --> 00:13:42,600
Yes.

275
00:13:42,840 --> 00:13:46,200
But so the effect of this—and I think
this is not being well followed...

276
00:13:46,879 --> 00:13:47,879
Paid attention to in the world.

277
00:13:47,964 --> 00:13:50,800
But, the incredible

278
00:13:51,759 --> 00:13:55,915
exponential of how much compute you can

279
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,480
throw at a single problem and get
better results is one thing.

280
00:13:58,960 --> 00:14:03,156
The delta between what most people are
using AI for, and what the people who are

281
00:14:03,240 --> 00:14:06,755
using AI the most productively for has
just continued to spread,

282
00:14:06,840 --> 00:14:08,096
and spread, and spread.

283
00:14:08,681 --> 00:14:12,440
How do you allocate compute budgets now?

284
00:14:12,759 --> 00:14:16,995
Because presumably, even though
researchers are paid a lot,

285
00:14:17,080 --> 00:14:18,759
it's eclipsed by compute.

286
00:14:19,320 --> 00:14:24,836
And so, capital allocation at OpenAI
is sort of compute allocation.

287
00:14:24,919 --> 00:14:28,240
And it's hard to a priori know

288
00:14:29,399 --> 00:14:32,035
how much it will cost to go do something.
Yeah.

289
00:14:32,120 --> 00:14:34,875
It's actually also hard to just
control the...

290
00:14:34,960 --> 00:14:38,676
You can't quite prompt an agent to where
it's like: "Go do a really good job of

291
00:14:38,759 --> 00:14:41,596
this and don't spend more than $10,000."
You can.

292
00:14:41,679 --> 00:14:42,799
Oh, okay.
Will it do a good job of it?

293
00:14:42,884 --> 00:14:46,235
Yeah, if you say do the best job
you can with $10,000 of compute.

294
00:14:46,320 --> 00:14:48,805
Yes, it will understand that.
Huh.

295
00:14:48,919 --> 00:14:49,959
I mean, there's a...

296
00:14:50,043 --> 00:14:53,196
There's another kind of hard question
for society about what does it mean

297
00:14:53,279 --> 00:14:54,515
when you have two companies competing.

298
00:14:54,600 --> 00:14:57,600
And one says you can spend $10,000 and one
says you can spend $100,000

299
00:14:57,683 --> 00:14:58,779
on the same problem.

300
00:14:59,240 --> 00:15:00,320
It's clear what's going to happen there.

301
00:15:00,403 --> 00:15:04,836
But there's a question about how society
thinks about compute allocation,

302
00:15:04,919 --> 00:15:06,875
and wealth, and things like that.

303
00:15:06,960 --> 00:15:09,139
But we have our own version
of this inside of OpenAI.

304
00:15:09,960 --> 00:15:15,235
We allocate a lot of compute
to internal productivity.

305
00:15:15,320 --> 00:15:20,760
And then people get very wide latitude
to make their own decisions,

306
00:15:20,960 --> 00:15:22,680
unless they're really being abusive.

307
00:15:23,798 --> 00:15:25,799
But do you encourage
people to do useful stuff?

308
00:15:25,919 --> 00:15:28,915
And if they spend $10 million in compute,
it's like: "Oh, interesting."

309
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:29,360
Correct.

310
00:15:29,443 --> 00:15:31,235
We try never to reward people
for using a lot of compute.

311
00:15:31,320 --> 00:15:32,520
Sure.
No token leaderboards.

312
00:15:32,840 --> 00:15:36,396
It's actually slightly bad
to be high on the token leaderboard.

313
00:15:36,480 --> 00:15:37,940
Certainly on a promotion.

314
00:15:39,320 --> 00:15:43,600
But it's a skeptical default,
and if you can show that you're doing

315
00:15:43,720 --> 00:15:46,720
something important,
you will have nothing but appreciation.

316
00:15:47,113 --> 00:15:49,040
But at a certain point,
this will need to change.

317
00:15:49,124 --> 00:15:51,960
Because you're describing
the increasing orders of magnitude.

318
00:15:52,399 --> 00:15:56,435
And, right now you have a researcher
who does some very interesting

319
00:15:56,519 --> 00:15:57,795
math for $10 million.

320
00:15:57,879 --> 00:16:02,519
And you're like: "A little pricey,
but cool math." And then $100 million.

321
00:16:02,720 --> 00:16:05,636
When they're doing the $1 billion math,
you're like: "Look, I'm really happy

322
00:16:05,720 --> 00:16:07,076
that you're making so much math progress.

323
00:16:07,159 --> 00:16:09,500
But I really need you to cool
the jets with the math."

324
00:16:09,639 --> 00:16:12,155
Look, we did let it get out
of control for one week on the math.

325
00:16:12,240 --> 00:16:14,519
Intentionally so, because you have to—
And it worked!

326
00:16:14,679 --> 00:16:16,235
Well, you don't know how...

327
00:16:16,320 --> 00:16:18,756
You don't know how far it's
going to go until you try.

328
00:16:18,840 --> 00:16:20,875
Yeah.
And I think it's important to have...

329
00:16:20,960 --> 00:16:24,076
When there's a new paradigm,
I think it's important to let

330
00:16:24,159 --> 00:16:26,039
people be fairly unconstrained.

331
00:16:26,440 --> 00:16:31,156
So, when we did the Navier-Stokes proof,
we understood absolutely nothing about

332
00:16:31,240 --> 00:16:33,039
the scaling laws of teams of agents.

333
00:16:33,960 --> 00:16:35,235
Are you supposed to, like—

334
00:16:35,320 --> 00:16:37,379
The very recent Navier-Stokes proof?
Yes.

335
00:16:37,519 --> 00:16:39,879
Are you supposed to give
them the smartest model?

336
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,956
Are you supposed to give them many copies,
fewer copies, longer rollouts,

337
00:16:43,039 --> 00:16:45,179
shorter rollouts?
We did not understand that at all.

338
00:16:45,919 --> 00:16:48,759
And now, we understand it a little bit.

339
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,080
I think we can still
understand it much better.

340
00:16:51,163 --> 00:16:54,480
But like has happened in every other new
paradigm we've discovered,

341
00:16:55,472 --> 00:16:58,759
I am confident that we will find our 10x

342
00:16:59,519 --> 00:17:02,439
improvements per year at how we allocate

343
00:17:02,559 --> 00:17:06,319
dollars or computer flops, whatever you
want to measure, into useful things.

344
00:17:06,759 --> 00:17:09,680
So, I think this is a thing that
our company is particularly good at,

345
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,480
but also fits an important
property of the field.

346
00:17:12,599 --> 00:17:17,039
Which is: let people explore
at the limit of what is possible.

347
00:17:17,519 --> 00:17:21,000
And then trust that the
incredible optimization pressure

348
00:17:21,599 --> 00:17:24,099
will figure out how to do this
in an efficient way.

349
00:17:26,480 --> 00:17:32,716
Directionally speaking, I would rather us
be more willing to let people use crazy

350
00:17:32,799 --> 00:17:37,115
amounts of compute
and let users have extremely high-end

351
00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:40,476
plans to figure out
the edge of the possible.

352
00:17:40,559 --> 00:17:43,155
And then figure out how to make
that efficient and at mass scale.

353
00:17:43,240 --> 00:17:45,259
But when does the optimization
function come in?

354
00:17:45,690 --> 00:17:52,559
Well, I think we're already doing it.
I bet we could redo Navier-Stokes

355
00:17:53,480 --> 00:17:56,839
at 1/100th of the cost
a year from when it was done.

356
00:17:57,359 --> 00:17:57,959
So we did it in—

357
00:17:58,044 --> 00:18:00,997
Not just knowing how the proof works,
but just with better approaches.

358
00:18:01,400 --> 00:18:03,060
Yes.
Smarter models and better approaches.

359
00:18:03,160 --> 00:18:04,580
How much shall the math cost?

360
00:18:04,799 --> 00:18:06,036
Order $10 million per...

361
00:18:06,119 --> 00:18:07,439
For Navier-Stokes, I think.

362
00:18:08,480 --> 00:18:09,480
Okay.
Maybe a little more.

363
00:18:09,564 --> 00:18:11,316
$10 million-ish.

364
00:18:11,400 --> 00:18:13,355
That order of magnitude.
Yeah, yeah.

365
00:18:13,440 --> 00:18:14,556
But for one proof.

366
00:18:14,640 --> 00:18:15,920
And so—Well, no, no.

367
00:18:16,519 --> 00:18:17,960
Because then we kept
getting better so fast.

368
00:18:18,044 --> 00:18:19,716
We understood more.
Got it.

369
00:18:19,799 --> 00:18:22,395
We spent way less than
that on the average one of those.

370
00:18:22,480 --> 00:18:24,519
But like 100s of millions
of dollars of math.

371
00:18:24,880 --> 00:18:25,236
Yeah.
Yeah, yeah.

372
00:18:25,319 --> 00:18:25,836
That kind of work.

373
00:18:25,920 --> 00:18:26,480
Okay, interesting.

374
00:18:26,939 --> 00:18:31,956
But that includes much more other
discovery than what we have published.

375
00:18:32,039 --> 00:18:32,119
Yes.

376
00:18:32,203 --> 00:18:33,596
So it was like,
"Maybe we spent that much—"

377
00:18:33,680 --> 00:18:35,320
We have more than the 700 proofs?
We do.

378
00:18:35,440 --> 00:18:36,076
Of course.

379
00:18:36,160 --> 00:18:36,940
When did you get those?

380
00:18:38,701 --> 00:18:40,355
We are trying to learn how to...

381
00:18:40,440 --> 00:18:41,759
This is a real tension.

382
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:46,635
I think on one hand,
very clearly, and I feel unequivocal

383
00:18:46,720 --> 00:18:50,196
on this point:
Humanity deserves fundamental knowledge.

384
00:18:50,279 --> 00:18:55,799
And we should not be sitting on a major
fundamental science discovery.

385
00:18:57,960 --> 00:19:01,196
The world deserves
that and gets to know it.

386
00:19:01,279 --> 00:19:05,000
And I think that outweighs another very
important equity,

387
00:19:05,519 --> 00:19:09,639
which is: If you are a scientist,

388
00:19:10,599 --> 00:19:12,596
your career and a significant amount

389
00:19:12,680 --> 00:19:15,236
of your enjoyment and fulfillment
in life comes from discovering science.

390
00:19:15,319 --> 00:19:15,720
Yeah.

391
00:19:15,804 --> 00:19:19,400
And so if we're going to
continue to do this for math

392
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:25,000
or begin to do this for other fields,
then I think we more than owe it to

393
00:19:25,720 --> 00:19:32,476
the people that work in those fields
to figure out what is the solution space

394
00:19:32,559 --> 00:19:34,440
where the world gets to benefit
from the knowledge,

395
00:19:34,523 --> 00:19:37,679
and they also get the best version
of the future that they can have.

396
00:19:37,920 --> 00:19:39,796
So we are working through that right now.

397
00:19:39,880 --> 00:19:40,280
I did not...

398
00:19:40,364 --> 00:19:42,420
I don't know, I think
you may be too contrite.

399
00:19:42,559 --> 00:19:46,919
I did not interpret that moment as
particularly bad for AI where...

400
00:19:48,319 --> 00:19:51,279
I think everyone saw
the Mathematicians Union up in arms.

401
00:19:51,799 --> 00:19:55,955
And I think everyone else said, "Yeah,
I mean, have you paid attention to 2026?"

402
00:19:56,039 --> 00:19:57,519
As I said, I don't
think it was bad for AI.

403
00:19:57,604 --> 00:20:02,451
And I think, on net, the world deserves
to understand how the universe works.

404
00:20:03,240 --> 00:20:04,980
But I do have empathy for the method.

405
00:20:05,079 --> 00:20:07,119
I think we need to do this.
I think we should do this.

406
00:20:07,203 --> 00:20:10,076
I think if we can go understand...

407
00:20:10,160 --> 00:20:15,080
A theory of all of physics,
or how to cure a bunch of diseases.

408
00:20:15,200 --> 00:20:17,460
Clearly, that is knowledge
the world should have.

409
00:20:18,440 --> 00:20:20,875
But,
I would like to figure out a way to do

410
00:20:20,960 --> 00:20:26,436
this
that is as sensitive to the communities

411
00:20:26,519 --> 00:20:29,099
that have driven these fields
forward for centuries as we can.

412
00:20:29,440 --> 00:20:31,272
And it's just a...
It's a hard thing.

413
00:20:31,960 --> 00:20:33,836
I wasn't that worried about the math.
I thought it was great.

414
00:20:33,920 --> 00:20:35,680
But you agree the mathematicians
were not that happy?

415
00:20:35,776 --> 00:20:37,920
Yeah.
But, that's fine.

416
00:20:38,319 --> 00:20:41,995
Like, the longshoremen union weren't
happy about the port automation.

417
00:20:42,079 --> 00:20:43,241
Remember the guy?

418
00:20:44,039 --> 00:20:45,559
No, no, no, no, no.
I do.

419
00:20:45,799 --> 00:20:47,235
Why do I feel differently about this?

420
00:20:47,319 --> 00:20:49,720
There was one weekend when
we got a new model and in...

421
00:20:50,332 --> 00:20:53,396
I'm actually probably not supposed to say
this, but I will since we're drinking—

422
00:20:53,480 --> 00:20:54,115
Exactly.
We're a half pint in.

423
00:20:54,200 --> 00:20:54,476
Beers.
Yeah.

424
00:20:54,559 --> 00:20:55,200
Perfect time for—

425
00:20:55,284 --> 00:21:01,836
The name of the channel was: "Model
Name-Hold My Beer." And people were just

426
00:21:01,920 --> 00:21:05,000
throwing that at the hardest
problems they could.

427
00:21:05,597 --> 00:21:07,960
And people didn't think the model
was going to do these things.

428
00:21:08,044 --> 00:21:11,040
And we had this collective experience
of people's hardest research

429
00:21:11,124 --> 00:21:13,556
problems from their academic career.

430
00:21:13,640 --> 00:21:15,596
The model was just knocking
down one after the other.

431
00:21:15,680 --> 00:21:17,980
Many different fields.
It was incredible to watch.

432
00:21:18,279 --> 00:21:21,316
And two nights in a row,
I was up most of the night because I

433
00:21:21,400 --> 00:21:23,115
was so excited I couldn't go to bed.

434
00:21:23,200 --> 00:21:24,395
It was...

435
00:21:24,480 --> 00:21:28,328
Many of those problems that we published,
many other things.

436
00:21:29,329 --> 00:21:31,439
And I was tremendously excited and happy.

437
00:21:32,839 --> 00:21:38,000
But I also had much more sadness
and melancholy, and a feeling of

438
00:21:38,640 --> 00:21:40,840
something ending than I was expecting.

439
00:21:41,359 --> 00:21:43,519
So again, I agree.

440
00:21:44,640 --> 00:21:47,598
This is the right thing,
on behalf of the world.

441
00:21:47,839 --> 00:21:50,715
And also, I agree that this is just
the shape of reality and nothing

442
00:21:50,799 --> 00:21:52,399
that anyone can stop, or should stop.

443
00:21:53,119 --> 00:21:57,000
But, I do have real—

444
00:21:57,960 --> 00:22:00,196
I think it's a wistfulness
and nostalgia you can feel.

445
00:22:00,279 --> 00:22:01,319
It's more than nostalgia.

446
00:22:01,960 --> 00:22:02,440
Wistfulness.

447
00:22:03,113 --> 00:22:03,860
Yeah.

448
00:22:04,440 --> 00:22:07,680
My dad describes how,
growing up in Ireland in the 1950s,

449
00:22:08,160 --> 00:22:11,875
he observed people around him still
using horses productively, in industry.

450
00:22:11,960 --> 00:22:12,620
And—

451
00:22:13,359 --> 00:22:14,639
In the '50s?
Yeah.

452
00:22:14,960 --> 00:22:18,600
And I mean, not obviously, there were also
tractors, but they were being phased out.

453
00:22:20,200 --> 00:22:21,600
But it still for sure happened.

454
00:22:22,240 --> 00:22:27,279
And, observing that this was a technology
that humans had

455
00:22:28,279 --> 00:22:33,000
been using for thousands of years, and he
was witnessing the very tail end of it.

456
00:22:33,400 --> 00:22:37,956
And yeah, I think there's always
that feeling of witnessing the closing

457
00:22:38,039 --> 00:22:41,317
of a chapter that leads to some
wistfulness, but it's okay.

458
00:22:41,599 --> 00:22:44,719
It's people's lives and it's something
that has brought them great joy.

459
00:22:44,804 --> 00:22:47,036
And maybe most...

460
00:22:47,119 --> 00:22:51,835
I mean certainly, most mathematicians
never solve a Millennium Prize problem.

461
00:22:51,920 --> 00:22:53,960
But the idea that you might be able to.

462
00:22:55,079 --> 00:22:57,919
And the idea that there is stuff out there
that we don't understand yet,

463
00:22:58,003 --> 00:23:01,915
that through great effort and...

464
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,640
Pushing aside other things in your life
and dedicating yourself to,

465
00:23:04,723 --> 00:23:09,262
"You can still solve and understand."
There's something beautiful about that.

466
00:23:09,720 --> 00:23:11,740
What else are you excited about
from a safety point of view?

467
00:23:12,119 --> 00:23:13,399
We talked about this a little bit earlier.

468
00:23:13,483 --> 00:23:15,519
But I want to emphasize it again.

469
00:23:15,680 --> 00:23:19,000
I'll just call it swarms since
that seems like the term right now.

470
00:23:19,640 --> 00:23:23,759
I do think the properties of swarms
match a lot of the properties of

471
00:23:24,319 --> 00:23:26,839
the resilience of society,
